Prurigo Nodularis
Idiopathic & Benign Entities
Intensely pruritic firm hyperkeratotic nodules, Symmetrical extensor surface distribution, Violent uncontrollable picking, Butterfly sign (sparing of unreachable back)

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: IBE-043
Reason for Visit: Severe, unrelenting generalized itching and violent, uncontrollable skin picking resulting in bleeding and absolute insomnia.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage identified a patient suffering from intense pruritus leading to severe self-inflicted skin trauma, acute psychological distress, and complete sleep disruption over 48 hours. Urgent after-hours assessment was required to definitively rule out acute systemic allergic reaction or rapidly spreading secondary bacterial infection, and to provide immediate, highly potent topical and sedating therapy to forcibly break the destructive itch-scratch cycle prior to daytime primary care availability.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "I can't stop digging at these hard, itchy bumps on my arms. I know I'm bleeding but the itch is so deep."
History of Present Illness: Patient presents with a 48-hour severe exacerbation of extreme, unrelenting severe itching associated with the development of intensely pruritic, extremely firm, dome-shaped cutaneous nodules. The patient reports violent, uncontrollable picking and deep scratching of the lesions. The symptoms have caused chronic, deeply frustrating sleep disruption and high anxiety. The patient notes a complete absence of a distinct primary generalized dermatological rash prior to the localized scratching. Symptoms flared recently due to severe underlying psychological stress.
Functional Status: Highly agitated, actively picking at scabs on the arms during the interview.
Social/Safety Concerns: Extreme sleep deprivation; high risk of widespread secondary bacterial skin infection from deep, violent excoriations.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 135/85 | HR: 90 | Temp: 36.8 | RR: 16 | SpO2: 100% | Wt: 68 Kg
General: Exhausted, visibly anxious, continuous picking behavior.
Physical Exam: Dermatological examination reveals symmetrically distributed, distinct, extremely firm, hyperkeratotic dome-shaped nodules predominantly involving the extensor surfaces of the arms, lower legs, and upper back. The nodules have a distinctly scaly and deeply excoriated surface with hemorrhagic crusts. Noticeable sparing of the mid-upper back (the 'butterfly sign') is present, strictly indicating the absolute requirement of mechanical reaching to induce the lesions. No spreading cellulitic erythema, fluctuance, or foul-smelling purulent discharge.
Environmental Observations: Bloody tissues near the bed from severe scratching.
Medication Reconciliation: Attempted standard over-the-counter moisturizers and non-sedating antihistamines with zero efficacy.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Prurigo Nodularis - Status: Severe acute flare with excoriation
Differentials:

Scabies (Nodular) - Ruled out by the lack of characteristic burrows in the web spaces, the chronicity of the firm hyperkeratotic lesions, and the prominent butterfly sign sparing the back.

Bullous Pemphigoid - Ruled out by the absolute absence of tense fluid-filled blisters or mucosal involvement; the primary lesions are solid, hyperkeratotic nodules.

Secondary Bacterial Cellulitis - Ruled out by the complete absence of spreading warmth, deep induration, foul-smelling purulent discharge, or systemic fever.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Moderate

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient required an urgent after-hours assessment due to severe, intractable localized pruritus resulting in profound sleep deprivation, deep self-inflicted skin trauma, and acute psychological collapse. The severity of the bleeding and excoriations mandated an immediate clinical examination to definitively rule out a rapidly spreading secondary bacterial infection or highly infectious nodular scabies. Clinically confirming the chronic, mechanically induced nature of the thickened nodules allowed the physician to immediately prescribe ultra-potent topical steroids and sedating antihistamines to forcibly break the violent itch-scratch cycle, effectively preventing a highly distressed emergency department admission and satisfying the criteria for MBS Item 597.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Prescribed an ultra-potent topical corticosteroid (Betamethasone dipropionate 0.05% ointment) to aggressively reduce the deep localized inflammation. Administered oral Promethazine 25mg stat to provide immediate H1-receptor blockade and potent nocturnal sedation to prevent unconscious picking.
Positive Behavioral Change: Instead of violently picking the nodules when the intense itch occurs, aggressively apply a medical-grade ice pack directly over the thickest nodule for 5 minutes. The extreme cold directly overloads the deep sensory nerve fibers, completely shutting off the severe itch signal without physically destroying the skin, immediately rewarding the patient with profound relief and allowing the deep tissue to heal.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None.
DME/Safety (Optional): Cotton gloves for sleeping; occlusive dressings.
Follow-up: Routine GP follow-up in 1 week to monitor skin healing, monitor for infection, and consider referral to dermatology.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate ED presentation if developing spreading red streaks from the scratched nodules, a high systemic fever, or if the wounds begin leaking thick, foul-smelling yellow pus.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis relies on the absence of acute infection; if the deeply excoriated areas become hot and swollen, systemic antibiotic therapy will be immediately required.
Clinical Course: Prurigo nodularis is a self-perpetuating cycle where scratching causes the skin to form thick, hard nodules, which trap highly reactive nerves that cause even more intense itching. The skin will only heal and the itching will only stop when the mechanical scratching is absolutely ceased.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Seek immediate medical attention if you develop a high fever, if the redness spreads rapidly away from the hard bumps, or if the sores start oozing thick, yellow pus.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: The skin has formed incredibly hard, thick domes to protect itself from the chronic picking. Inside those domes, the nerves are inflamed and screaming. An ultra-strong prescription ointment under a dressing must be used to punch through the hard skin and shut down those nerves, combined with a sedative to stop picking during sleep.
Actionable Steps: Apply the strong ointment tonight and cover the worst nodules tightly with a band-aid or hydrocolloid patch. This physical barrier absolutely prevents the fingernails from digging into the nodule during sleep, breaking the cycle of tissue damage and directly reinforcing rapid healing.

